#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/id #uuid "1ecd84f8-5157-595a-93b6-a865acaa53d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8378" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n![](images/3b/3bf74b3f4c11508b9daa66464cc451933f5637c22ffeaaae303378fca2571893.png)\nand the curve $\\mathcal C,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+ 2x_2^2 -2x_1+2x_2 =7 .\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $5y_1^2-y_2^2,$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $\\mathcal C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\: \\underbrace{ 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2}_{Q(\\mathbf{x})} -2x_1+2x_2 =7\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/78/783b32a7595534982e5fb057c049081564807b9d47988670c7d30058fb3900a0.png)\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(\\mathbf x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable $y_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ 5{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/id #uuid "c03c6fbb-176a-5f30-b928-73908cb93277" :question/math-academy-id "e-8380" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ![](images/e6/e60bf9596889dbea2f6719078ad7817e12f96063cae887630abdd448e132437e.svg)-plane?\n\nI. ![](images/3d/3da52050877a2855dba7355e4bbdd91e95ad7cd31fe62ff8d5d0c6ca895f6589.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/b0/b0aa9bc434b0d9350630088f346e42243e24219ee4086b0b3563e6cc64bd552f.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/17/17750e29fd96388982c719635201ad4788c2c6d40352b96d3daba019fce8db12.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.  \n\n- The equation ![](images/3d/3da52050877a2855dba7355e4bbdd91e95ad7cd31fe62ff8d5d0c6ca895f6589.svg) defines a non-empty set. It represents the point   ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) in the ![](images/e1/e15fa72f0cdfc4bb75a38b1aa53e99baad6ed04b742e19b31d9c80f809bbd26e.svg)-plane.\n\n- The equation ![](images/b0/b0aa9bc434b0d9350630088f346e42243e24219ee4086b0b3563e6cc64bd552f.svg) defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ![](images/8c/8c33e6564649c3beca9591e67622629ffab451061b8a92931451ef854d188bcd.svg) and ![](images/f0/f0b10bbcfd13006bc3490f782396e26677fa5c329dd8a770c828aa9ca9011eb3.svg) while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ![](images/17/17750e29fd96388982c719635201ad4788c2c6d40352b96d3daba019fce8db12.svg) defines a non-empty set. It represents a  pair of intersecting lines, ![](images/01/012aab369190f18204bc3b4530eb5fd7eed60908a2fa25a7cfd3dac1f9d76402.svg) and ![](images/ee/eefefdeef49074edb3f06e7072ba2fc144ade0c05c90510b72b0c8d4eb0871ed.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/id #uuid "58f6a3e7-e867-576a-ad84-0debbbc38d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-8381" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3x_1^2 + 2x_1 - x_2 =\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the variable $x_1\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & ={x}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2} & ={y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive $y_2$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f341806-925a-5846-af56-fd1b87a9b475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556504} {:answer/id #uuid "81ba458d-d2c7-5ebb-a9fb-2081c6240003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556730} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1dc190-ff97-5bda-8ac3-23bb02de7b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556813} {:answer/id #uuid "b5857968-ffe1-5ebd-b666-1ec59aa4ad66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556873} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6c4fbf-1a26-53cc-8b14-4d4f5435eed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f341806-925a-5846-af56-fd1b87a9b475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556504} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaca67a9-c11b-5ef7-9bd5-650d3e06d0b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0f59d2f-ea36-559d-85fe-96af7c52c659" :question/math-academy-id "q-140553" :question/problem "Which of the following equations defines two intersecting lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines two intersecting lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define two parallel lines:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=3$ defines two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86538b9a-7568-5933-8854-c5b4658f48fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468942} {:answer/id #uuid "7015220a-f9e7-5df7-8058-44ffcd7f8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7015220a-f9e7-5df7-8058-44ffcd7f8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468943} :answer-field/id #uuid "1042346b-f6d0-5bb9-9f5f-bce0a807b4a3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470083} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec93fa16-4cde-5228-9add-72224548a4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468944} {:answer/id #uuid "f37fedd9-c61b-5bc4-9e9b-a9572e1053ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37fedd9-c61b-5bc4-9e9b-a9572e1053ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468945} :answer-field/id #uuid "862e7db4-e4cf-5fa7-beaa-ce2dadf572fd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f6328b-6e1c-5742-b270-297de5ba0087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468946} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2783d8-30e3-52a0-8485-391ca3a4046f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2783d8-30e3-52a0-8485-391ca3a4046f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468947} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91acfa1-f88a-5755-910e-8b8e92796d63" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7ec5e81-6115-5881-b6f7-16bb9226da5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140554" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$\n- **III.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$ defines a non-empty set. It represents the hyperbola ${x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b2f2de-15d8-5ab1-b00e-502c92c80ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365019} {:answer/id #uuid "4a07a491-9472-50b7-8caa-2551ff3dffe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365020} {:answer/id #uuid "d3feab1f-710d-5f7d-916d-6dccaf02ac63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365021} {:answer/id #uuid "626e74b3-c07c-5db1-8452-88dfd54f5e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365022} {:answer/id #uuid "83cebc46-9365-5662-a211-861041f69667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "626e74b3-c07c-5db1-8452-88dfd54f5e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365022} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa02b4c6-e130-5f57-94ca-7875e4b08f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59ff20d8-dfd7-5f57-8dd7-676de1c62793" :question/math-academy-id "q-140557" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}=-7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1} & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4) & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variable\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2,\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff7e060b-1316-5b07-b470-df5ef86724ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366617} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c4007f-a76f-5450-9d48-38785eca112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366618} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb12856-ee48-5173-af4f-f74503391dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366619} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d1a16b-ebea-596a-b098-e79157afe072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366620} {:answer/id #uuid "43f64814-08c5-5d87-94c2-ca5d06ca5917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c4007f-a76f-5450-9d48-38785eca112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366618} :answer-field/id #uuid "204f44fd-2c19-5e76-8874-9a7fd60fd729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c86266f7-94c1-52a6-b432-a64ea0eb634a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95391" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})+3\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+10{y}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1) & =8+1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c543b2-c742-5216-a976-704265096bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556437} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58c25d-4003-58d6-ba24-34ce3e87a8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556600} {:answer/id #uuid "e2716f9d-9b38-5af5-a1db-0a58828ce254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556989} {:answer/id #uuid "f08c1f85-9679-5c12-98ff-95ec5e74f12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557023} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b9bfec-c82b-508d-8c85-f0390ed2dd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $9$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08c1f85-9679-5c12-98ff-95ec5e74f12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557023} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e2b3fe-1fd8-5dda-acb7-cbb560deb0ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "584e6ba7-6a66-5994-b1b0-75bfa6a71313" :question/math-academy-id "q-95392" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ba80ef-f04e-50df-b6d3-a54349425c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $5$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556614} {:answer/id #uuid "99191248-3fa5-597a-8b54-a603b1f3f7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556797} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e84b15-e7c8-5fd4-aa6e-1f803de91be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556826} {:answer/id #uuid "da3da08e-ab18-5b95-a7d3-b32ef06eaf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556972} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ed344-c362-572c-a34e-eb57da043aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e84b15-e7c8-5fd4-aa6e-1f803de91be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556826} :answer-field/id #uuid "2872dfa0-d71b-51c3-93f9-e6e87fc08e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27762923-ddbc-55ca-b261-ca5c61feb064" :question/math-academy-id "q-95396" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c75de21-c317-5237-86ad-e88e440edb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404810} {:answer/id #uuid "84aa3f18-3554-54fd-ae6f-b39ef7e2bffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404811} {:answer/id #uuid "6840aa9b-2e07-5c84-8108-3e6b1673c189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404812} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa9bf77-da54-502a-8bbc-e943b623178f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404813} {:answer/id #uuid "41613630-2c51-5ec0-9667-5e99088f87b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41613630-2c51-5ec0-9667-5e99088f87b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404814} :answer-field/id #uuid "394f4d35-8eb9-5319-8076-2545b5e06b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c9fcd9c7-a9e3-5124-83fe-aab4333a0614" :question/math-academy-id "q-95399" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $10{y}_{1}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})-6\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}-20{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}+1)-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{z}_{1}={y}_{1}-1,\n$$\n\nwe get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the pair of parallel lines  ${z}_{1}=1$ and ${z}_{1}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26ebc2d-f69b-5be5-a62d-fe9979a136f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171352} {:answer/id #uuid "a7226782-b0b8-5d75-83ac-7f6e8629c23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171353} {:answer/id #uuid "c22bc215-d13d-59ed-b376-a61232d50fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171354} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab9b53c-e1b2-5002-844a-b3352768d4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171355} {:answer/id #uuid "575bdd47-ebf1-53ed-9f53-e33ef9a63b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171356}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f2036a-0fe0-50c3-b684-fe037208bf4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171351} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e68231-d9b2-5aa6-aa84-112d0e369b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382609} {:answer/id #uuid "c5acccd0-f6c1-52dc-be77-7297d98e1951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382610} {:answer/id #uuid "584525fe-a6ca-5466-a0ac-0215dce4e93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382611} {:answer/id #uuid "a71f511b-3fb2-554e-b7b1-c0c673c47df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382612} {:answer/id #uuid "b2730ecd-1e31-5200-83ad-515fcbe8bd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584525fe-a6ca-5466-a0ac-0215dce4e93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382611} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a166c0-af1b-5c41-95f1-54242ee0704a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba048324-ec24-5cc4-9fc3-2455f8f53e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-95400" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})-2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2}+25)-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2}-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})^{2}}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis defines an empty set, also called an imaginary ellipse. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c8aa32-aecd-58d0-b7aa-cb91d54665f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556537} {:answer/id #uuid "3b000012-fe46-5a84-bd3f-bad1806b55a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556558} {:answer/id #uuid "aeff25ea-7c7f-5e53-8336-a288b46933fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{3}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=2{x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556860} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1a628a-53f0-5a4e-b216-e3ef743b0eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556928} {:answer/id #uuid "ff73a978-082a-56ac-85c8-c77c4ca04e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b000012-fe46-5a84-bd3f-bad1806b55a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556558} :answer-field/id #uuid "39cdf548-2580-5d02-8060-91eecdc48cb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fe38749-b027-5e25-83ce-123ba8294cb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95701" :question/problem "Which of the following equations defines two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=7.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines two parallel lines, ${x}_{2}=\\sqrt{7}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\sqrt{7},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define two parallel lines:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=2{x}_{1}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f74e5cac-5fa1-5913-b097-bbfbb558a4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477491} {:answer/id #uuid "b04d30a9-862b-5922-aa61-4c21080e6261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b04d30a9-862b-5922-aa61-4c21080e6261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477492} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9331d7-eb32-5848-a41a-7768e9e3c9dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "739f9b2d-f525-52a6-9c4c-b5f7a895148d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477493} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb66722-f6bc-5c27-b93b-33f61576756f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "739f9b2d-f525-52a6-9c4c-b5f7a895148d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477493} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad460fe6-a6b8-5d19-8fcd-46fae2f7d401" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb480cd5-e9a4-517a-b0d5-4fcec4bff81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477495} {:answer/id #uuid "8a869b7d-6beb-5fe3-93e8-d4e81ef106e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb480cd5-e9a4-517a-b0d5-4fcec4bff81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477495} :answer-field/id #uuid "854812e6-d405-5148-8484-67f195d21ee7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "098d9a26-30b9-593f-93e1-d543e66fd2ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-95703" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$\n- **III.** ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$ defines a non-empty set. It represents two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8106517b-fb23-5d44-9d0b-3404c7d99c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556727} {:answer/id #uuid "838da181-4673-55e0-bdc0-360a05e94dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556736} {:answer/id #uuid "ca837b7f-fe36-5592-a3c4-d9f0cbc80e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556916} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc6a86f-507d-5ee4-b110-13e39242b587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557014} {:answer/id #uuid "f8322c23-6681-5083-a9c0-f4800c05f750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecc6a86f-507d-5ee4-b110-13e39242b587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557014} :answer-field/id #uuid "977b55e3-a317-5bd9-b4ac-64fc7f8737d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e624380-04d1-5614-ba5a-b0ac9bddf8d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95870" :question/problem "Which of the following equations defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis,  in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a parabola:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=4$ represents two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane: ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9905cbf0-c114-5744-928d-549e3bb50896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366727} {:answer/id #uuid "9e16170d-702e-59ac-b59e-0c7868a93b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366728} {:answer/id #uuid "80dbb7a6-0d32-5adc-8510-5b0aaf2c2838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366729} {:answer/id #uuid "8f72c2ee-efca-59b0-87ff-0f833655ad91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366730} {:answer/id #uuid "7a752195-1acf-532a-b58a-41a03894c235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f72c2ee-efca-59b0-87ff-0f833655ad91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366730} :answer-field/id #uuid "66665b04-8cc0-542f-ad9b-e8d5d6255ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d74d7da-0e93-513c-8a0b-cbe7ece164cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-96067" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=9\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}) & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}+9-9) & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-3-({x}_{2}-3)^{2}+9 & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-({x}_{2}-3)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-({x}_{2}-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{\\sqrt{3}}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01632674-02ee-5ccc-844f-443846c79b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intesecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556432} {:answer/id #uuid "08c8a772-ae09-512b-a890-92df6d1b8e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556449} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2cc041-c79e-5e86-a8d4-d48bf4faa510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556773} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8c1e08-78ee-5f92-b589-90b9f4d1a083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556776} {:answer/id #uuid "dd035faa-00da-5a95-8985-5b4ebe31a5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e2cc041-c79e-5e86-a8d4-d48bf4faa510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556773} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b32496-c7c6-5ba5-9d57-aa0f0c7fc355" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f46d525e-1765-5c37-bb92-276f989431a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96069" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=13\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2}+3 & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1 \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09493249-579f-50d6-83f8-d9f4339e49f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404873} {:answer/id #uuid "d2aec024-bd0a-5a8b-adfb-0e939c2e2b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404874} {:answer/id #uuid "78bda88f-4c26-582a-bfaf-2eedaeccc00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404875} {:answer/id #uuid "e6eb1689-82d7-59cd-87a7-f9f401986eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404876} {:answer/id #uuid "d21ffd0e-5191-5fa0-b217-96304319a883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21ffd0e-5191-5fa0-b217-96304319a883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404877} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5aaa14-82c5-5b2c-8c17-18030cfdb9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d0bc05c-84b7-5858-84df-86a5d9c6d43e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96084" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}-1)^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =-({x}_{1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-2, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{2}^{2} & =-{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${y}_{1}$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c580589-f052-5d9b-a6c6-7fbb1e3611bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556454} {:answer/id #uuid "3a943015-36f4-5c0a-95ff-665bc2f267cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556556} {:answer/id #uuid "5232f5b8-0b2a-5331-a3c6-6bdf904e28d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556617} {:answer/id #uuid "80046462-96bc-5593-af9e-f1048b0a9e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556724} {:answer/id #uuid "91de276f-566d-5d21-823b-f4eec78a0be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5232f5b8-0b2a-5331-a3c6-6bdf904e28d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556617} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d11b4fd-cfd0-5513-851a-592175c62665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f72287c6-b1eb-590a-839e-db294cf79f90" :question/math-academy-id "q-96091" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a circle of radius $1.$"}]}
